{
  "program": "COUSR02C",
  "caseId": "case20",
  "description": "The WHEN OTHER arm of the ordered EVALUATE EIBAID at app/cbl/COUSR02C.cbl:127-130, reached with DFHPF9 - a key the program names in none of its five explicit arms. The arm sets the error flag, moves CCDA-MSG-INVALID-KEY into WS-MESSAGE and sends the screen once.\n\nThe message is where the channel distinction bites. CSMSG01Y declares CCDA-MSG-INVALID-KEY as PIC X(50), so the literal 'Invalid key pressed. Please see below...' arrives space-padded to fifty; the MOVE at :129 puts it into WS-MESSAGE PIC X(80), padding to eighty; and the MOVE at :270 puts THAT into ERRMSGO PIC X(78), truncating two bytes. The screen therefore carries exactly seventy-eight characters, and this case states them as a send payload on the seventy-eight-byte screen channel rather than as a free-standing line of unstated width. The two lost bytes are spaces here, but they are lost, and only an expectation that names its channel can show that.\n\nNo cursor is pinned: this arm moves -1 into no length item, so the cursor is wherever the map left it. No attribute is pinned either - the arm moves no colour, which is itself worth noticing, since every other error path in the program does.",
